{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794474268,"nonce":"GOXZrX4QQybZNdhPQxslH6XBugs=","message":"The shipping and platform gaps from the same request on 2026-11-12: a package that haxelib takes, a library built for a machine other than the one packaging it, and whether any of it works on a phone.\n\n**`--target \u003ctriple\u003e`** on `hxn build cpp` and `hxn package` builds the hxcpp binding for another machine. It is a `staticlib`, so cargo needs the target's standard library and no linker: `rustup target add` is all it takes. From this Mac it built for `aarch64-apple-ios`, `x86_64-apple-ios` and `aarch64-linux-android` with nothing else set. An `.hdll` is a `cdylib`, linked when built, so `--target` with `hl` is refused with that reason; so are `run` and `check`, which would have to run the result. The triple is read once and leaked, to keep `Target` a `Copy` value.\n\n**`hxn.Bind` picks a phone's library from hxcpp's own defines**: `iphoneos` gives `aarch64-apple-ios`; `iphonesim` gives `x86_64-apple-ios`, or `aarch64-apple-ios-sim` with `HXCPP_ARM64`; `android` gives the triple of `HXCPP_ARM64`, `HXCPP_X86_64`, `HXCPP_X86` or, with none of those, `armv7-linux-androideabi`.\n\n**What was found on a phone's toolchain**, with hxcpp 4.3.2, Xcode 26.4 and NDK 27.0.12077973:\n\n- iOS: hxcpp builds an archive, and its `libtool` step puts the library's archive into it, so an app links one file. `hxRunLibrary()` after `hxcpp_set_top_of_stack()` starts it. hxcpp 4.3.2 builds a simulator's library for i386 or, with `HXCPP_M64`, x86-64, and for nothing else, so on Apple silicon the simulator runs it through Rosetta. All six examples print what their `expected.txt` lists in an iPhone 15 simulator on iOS 17.5. Not run: a device, and an arm64 simulator, which this hxcpp does not build for.\n- Android: hxcpp builds `libMain-64.so` with the archive linked in. Two things are hxcpp's and the NDK's to settle and are worked around in the test, not in hxn: hxcpp looks for the NDK's tools under `darwin-x86` where the NDK has `darwin-x86_64` (`-D ANDROID_HOST=`), and strips with `aarch64-linux-android-strip`, which this NDK does not have (`-D HXCPP_DEBUG_LINK`). A program that links the library and calls `hxcpp_main()` runs from `/data/local/tmp`; it is C, since a C++ one asks for `libc++_shared.so`. What it prints goes to the device's log under the tag `trace`. All six examples pass on an arm64 emulator on Android 16 (API 36). Not run: a device, and the library loaded by Java in an app.\n- `tests/mobile/run.sh ios-sim|android` does this for every example. A panic and the fixture are not run on either.\n\n**`haxelib.json`** lists what the crate gives: `url` from `repository` or `homepage`, `license` as the one of haxelib's names (GPL, LGPL, BSD, Public, MIT, Apache) the crate's expression starts with, `tags` from `keywords`, and `contributors` and `releasenote` from `[package.metadata.hxn]`. `package` prints what `haxelib submit` will still ask for. Nothing has been submitted.\n\n**CI** gained two jobs, neither of which has run: `emscripten` (the generated fixture and the examples, with emsdk 5.0.7 fetched) and `ios` (the examples in a simulator on `macos-latest`). The second depends on the runner having Rosetta and a simulator that runs x86-64, which is not known. Android has no job: it needs an emulator action and a machine that virtualizes.\n\nStill open:\n- Nothing is tagged or published: a new library depends on a checkout or a git URL.\n- `hxn run` and `hxn check` on a phone; a device of either kind.\n- An `.hdll` for another machine, which takes that machine's linker (zig, or a cross toolchain).\n- `hxn doctor` does not report which `--target`s are installed.\n\nCommitted as 43b244d and, for CI, ca8ea80. Not pushed.","files":null}]}